{"name":"Cantaloupe","hex":"#ffd479","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cantaloupe","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Cucumis melo Cantalupensis Group, a cultivar group melon with sweet aromatic orange flesh, including two main types:\n Cantalupensis Group sensu stricto, with non- or slightly netted rind, also known as true cantaloupe or European cantaloupe, found in the Middle East and Europe. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n Reticulatus Group (now merged into Cantalupensis Group), with strongly netted rind, also known as American cantaloupe, muskmelon or rockmelon.","Cantalupensis Group sensu stricto, with non- or slightly netted rind, also known as true cantaloupe or European cantaloupe, found in the Middle East and Europe. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cantaloupe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cantaloupe","blocks":[{"text":"The cantaloupe is a type of true melon with sweet, aromatic, and usually orange flesh. Originally, cantaloup referred to the true cantaloupe or European cantaloupe with non- to slightly netted and often ribbed rind. Today, it also refers to the muskmelon with strongly netted rind, which is called cantaloupe in North America, rockmelon in Australia and New Zealand, and spanspek in Southern Africa. Cantaloupes range in mass from 0.5 to 5 kilograms."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Meloen_vrucht_met_bloem.jpg/330px-Meloen_vrucht_met_bloem.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cantaloupe","wikidata":"Q477179"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Cucumis_melo_var._reticulatus_%28photo_by_Scott_Bauer%29.jpg/500px-Cucumis_melo_var._reticulatus_%28photo_by_Scott_Bauer%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu